\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Motivation}

The theory of complex multiplication supplies, for each imaginary quadratic discriminant $D < 0$, a monic polynomial $H_D \in \Z[X]$ --- the \emph{Hilbert class polynomial} --- whose complex roots are the \emph{singular moduli} of discriminant $D$, that is the values $j(\tau)$ of the modular $j$-function at the $h(D)$ classes of quadratic forms of discriminant $D$. Its degree is the class number $h(D)$. Small examples are strikingly concrete:
\begin{gather*}
H_{-4}(X) = X - 1728,\quad H_{-7}(X) = X + 3375,\quad H_{-8}(X) = X - 8000,\\
H_{-11}(X) = X + 32768,\quad H_{-19}(X) = X + 884736,\quad H_{-163}(X) = X + 262537412640768000,\\
H_{-15}(X) = X^2 + 191025X - 121287375,\quad H_{-20}(X) = X^2 - 1264000X - 681472000.
\end{gather*}

Reduction modulo a prime $p$ is governed by Deuring's theory: $H_D$ splits completely modulo $p$, with $h(D)$ distinct roots, precisely when $p$ is represented by a quadratic form of discriminant $D$, equivalently when $4p = u^2 + |D|v^2$ is solvable in integers. Each root is then the $j$-invariant of an elliptic curve over $\F_p$ having CM by the order of discriminant $D$.

This structure suggests an attack on a semiprime $N = pq$. If one could guess an integer $j_0$ that is a root of $H_D$ modulo $p$ but not modulo $q$, then $p \mid H_D(j_0)$ and $q \nmid H_D(j_0)$, so $\gcd(H_D(j_0), N) = p$: the unknown factor is recovered by a single gcd.

The method works. Its correctness requires no conjecture, and it factors real semiprimes: with $D = -11$ and $j_0 = 9$ one has $H_{-11}(9) = 32777$ and $\gcd(32777, 5183) = 73$, so $5183 = 71 \cdot 73$. The purpose of this paper is to determine, exactly and unconditionally, how the method scales.

\subsection{Summary of results}

Throughout, $N = pq$ with $p \ne q$ prime, and $H \in \Z[X]$ is an integer polynomial, monic where stated, of degree $h$; $r_m$ denotes the number of roots of $H$ in $\Z/m$.

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Exact criterion} (Theorem~\ref{thm:criterion}): $\gcd(H(j_0), N)$ is a nontrivial divisor of $N$ iff $j_0$ is a root of $H$ modulo exactly one of $p, q$; and then the gcd equals that prime.
\item \textbf{Exact count} (Theorem~\ref{thm:count}): the number of successful residues in $[0,N)$ is $S = r_p(q-r_q) + (p-r_p)r_q$.
\item \textbf{Density bound} (Theorems~\ref{thm:density} and~\ref{thm:densitybal}): $S \le h(p+q)$, hence $S/N \le h(1/p+1/q)$, and $S/N \le 4h/\sqrt N$ for balanced $N$.
\item \textbf{Two-sided scaling} (Theorem~\ref{thm:twosided}): if $r_p \ge 1$ and $r_q = 0$ then $\sqrt N/(4h) \le N/S \le \sqrt N$.
\item \textbf{Work barrier} (Theorem~\ref{thm:work}): $h \cdot (N/S) \ge \sqrt N/4$, independently of $h$.
\item \textbf{Composition and multi-discriminant barriers} (Theorems~\ref{thm:comp} and~\ref{thm:multi}).
\item \textbf{No precomputation} (Theorems~\ref{thm:precomp} and~\ref{thm:tablefails}).
\item \textbf{Ladder placement} (Theorem~\ref{thm:ladder}): $C_h(x) = e^{x/2}/(4h)$ is superpolynomial, is not subexponential, and eventually dominates $e^{x/4}$.
\item \textbf{Blindness} (Theorem~\ref{thm:blind}): if $H$ has no root modulo either prime, no evaluation point ever succeeds.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{What is not claimed}

None of this asserts that no method based on complex multiplication can factor quickly. What is proved is that the \emph{gcd-of-polynomial-values} scheme --- in the full generality of an arbitrary monic integer polynomial, arbitrary evaluation points, and arbitrary finite discriminant families --- is a $\sqrt N$ method. This is a contribution to the classification of factoring barriers, not a claim about the security of any deployed system.

\section{Setup and definitions}

\begin{definition}[Nontrivial divisor]
For $N \in \N$, a natural number $d$ is a \emph{nontrivial divisor} of $N$ if $d \mid N$, $1 < d$ and $d < N$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[The gcd step]
For $H \in \Z[X]$, $j \in \Z$ and $N \in \N$ set
\[ \eg(H, j, N) := \gcd\bigl(H(j),\, N\bigr) \in \N. \]
One \emph{evaluation} of the method is one computation of $\eg(H,j,N)$; it \emph{succeeds} if $\eg(H,j,N)$ is a nontrivial divisor of $N$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Root count]
For $m \ge 1$ let $\overline H \in (\Z/m)[X]$ be the reduction of $H$ modulo $m$, and put
\[ R_m(H) := \{x \in \Z/m : \overline H(x) = 0\}, \qquad r_m(H) := |R_m(H)|. \]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Success set and count]
\[ \mathcal S(H,N) := \{\, j \in [0,N) : \eg(H,j,N)\ \text{is a nontrivial divisor of } N \,\}, \qquad S(H,N) := |\mathcal S(H,N)|. \]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Balanced semiprime]
$N = pq$ is \emph{balanced} if $p \le q \le 3p$. Any fixed constant in place of $3$ changes only the constant $4$ below; RSA moduli are balanced in a much stronger sense.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Cost classes]\label{def:cost}
Let $x = \log N$ be the bit-size variable and $f : \R \to \R$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $f$ is \emph{polynomially bounded} if $f(x) = O(x^d)$ for some $d$;
\item $f$ is \emph{superpolynomial} if $x^d/f(x) \to 0$ as $x\to\infty$ for every $d$;
\item $f$ is \emph{subexponential} if $f(x)/e^{\varepsilon x} \to 0$ as $x\to\infty$ for every $\varepsilon > 0$.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

Thus $e^{cx}$ with $c>0$ is superpolynomial and not subexponential, while the sieve cost $L_N[1/3,c]$ is subexponential. The three benchmark rungs are $L_N[1/3,c]$ (number field sieve), $e^{x/4} = N^{1/4}$ (birthday/rho) and $e^{x/2} = \sqrt N$.

\section{The exact gcd criterion}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:gcd}
Let $p \ne q$ be primes and $a \in \Z$. Then
\[
\gcd(a, pq) =
\begin{cases}
1 & \text{if } p \nmid a \text{ and } q \nmid a,\\
p & \text{if } p \mid a \text{ and } q \nmid a,\\
q & \text{if } p \nmid a \text{ and } q \mid a,\\
pq & \text{if } p \mid a \text{ and } q \mid a.
\end{cases}
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Set $d = \gcd(a,pq)$; then $d \mid pq$, so $d \in \{1,p,q,pq\}$. In the second case, $p \mid a$ and $p \mid pq$ give $p \mid d$, say $d = pk$ with $k \mid q$; if $k = q$ then $q \mid d \mid a$, contradicting $q \nmid a$, so $k = 1$ and $d = p$. The third case follows by exchanging $p$ and $q$. In the fourth case, coprimality of $p$ and $q$ gives $pq \mid a$ and hence $pq \mid d$, so $d = pq$. The first case is the remaining possibility.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact success criterion]\label{thm:criterion}
Let $p \ne q$ be primes, $H \in \Z[X]$ and $j \in \Z$. Then $\eg(H,j,pq)$ is a nontrivial divisor of $pq$ if and only if
\[ \bigl(p \mid H(j)\bigr) \XOR \bigl(q \mid H(j)\bigr). \]
Moreover, in the first disjunct the gcd equals $p$, and in the second it equals $q$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:gcd} with $a = H(j)$. In the two exclusive cases the gcd is a prime factor, which is nontrivial since $1 < p < pq$. In the remaining cases the gcd is $1$ or $pq$, neither of which is a nontrivial divisor.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
A successful gcd step on a semiprime outputs one of the two prime factors, so a single success factors $N$ completely.
\end{corollary}

\begin{remark}[Too much success is failure]
If $j_0$ is a root of $H$ modulo \emph{both} primes, the gcd equals $N$ and no information is obtained. The success condition is genuinely an exclusive-or, not a disjunction --- a point that heuristic treatments frequently blur.
\end{remark}

\begin{theorem}[Blindness]\label{thm:blind}
If $H$ has no root modulo $p$ and no root modulo $q$, then $\eg(H,j,pq) = 1$ for every $j \in \Z$: the method never succeeds for that discriminant, however many evaluations are performed.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Immediate from the first case of Lemma~\ref{lem:gcd}.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:blind} is not a pathological corner. $H_D$ splits modulo $p$ only when $D$ is a square modulo $p$, and otherwise $H_D$ can be irreducible modulo $p$. In the experiments of Section~\ref{sec:exp}, the pair $(p,q) = (71,73)$ with $D = -23$ has $r_{71} = r_{73} = 0$ and exact success count $0$. Every expectation computation below is therefore \emph{conditional} on the discriminant being usable.

\section{Exact counting via the Chinese Remainder Theorem}

The success condition depends on $j$ only through $(j \bmod p,\, j \bmod q)$, which makes an exact count possible.

\begin{lemma}[Product counting]\label{lem:prod}
Let $A \subseteq \Z/p$ and $B \subseteq \Z/q$. Then
\[ \bigl|\{(a,b) : (a \in A) \XOR (b \in B)\}\bigr| = |A|(q - |B|) + (p - |A|)|B|. \]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The set is the disjoint union $(A \times B^c) \sqcup (A^c \times B)$, disjoint by the first coordinate. Take cardinalities.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[CRT transfer]\label{lem:crt}
Let $\gcd(p,q) = 1$ and let $R$ be any predicate on $\Z/p \times \Z/q$. Then
\[ \bigl|\{ j \in [0,pq) : R(j \bmod p,\, j \bmod q)\}\bigr| = \bigl|\{(a,b) : R(a,b)\}\bigr|. \]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The map $j \mapsto (j \bmod p, j \bmod q)$ on $[0,pq)$ is injective, since $j_1 \equiv j_2$ modulo both coprime moduli forces $j_1 \equiv j_2 \pmod{pq}$ and $0 \le j_i < pq$; it is surjective by the Chinese Remainder Theorem. It therefore restricts to a bijection between the two sets.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact success count]\label{thm:count}
Let $p \ne q$ be primes and $H \in \Z[X]$ arbitrary. Then
\[ S(H,pq) = r_p\,(q - r_q) + (p - r_p)\,r_q. \]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:criterion}, $j$ succeeds iff $(p \mid H(j)) \XOR (q \mid H(j))$, i.e.\ iff $(j \bmod p \in R_p) \XOR (j \bmod q \in R_q)$, since $m \mid H(j)$ says exactly that $j \bmod m$ is a root of $\overline H$. Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:crt} with this predicate and then Lemma~\ref{lem:prod} with $A = R_p$, $B = R_q$.
\end{proof}

Equivalently $S = r_p q + p r_q - 2 r_p r_q$, an inclusion--exclusion with no error term and no probabilistic input.

\begin{lemma}[Lagrange]\label{lem:lagrange}
If $H$ is monic of degree $h$ and $m$ is prime, then $r_m \le h$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Reduction preserves monicity, so $\overline H \in \F_m[X]$ is monic of degree $h$ and in particular nonzero; a nonzero polynomial over a field has at most $\deg$ roots.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[The useful set is tiny]\label{cor:tiny}
For monic $H$ of degree $h$ and distinct primes $p, q$, $S(H,pq) \le h(p+q)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
From Theorem~\ref{thm:count}, $S \le r_p q + p r_q \le hq + ph$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:lagrange}.
\end{proof}

\section{The $\sqrt{N}$ density barrier}

\begin{theorem}[Density bound]\label{thm:density}
For monic $H$ of degree $h$ and distinct primes $p,q$,
\[ \frac{S(H,pq)}{pq} \le h\Bigl(\frac1p + \frac1q\Bigr). \]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Divide Corollary~\ref{cor:tiny} by $pq$ and use $(p+q)/(pq) = 1/p + 1/q$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Balanced $\sqrt N$ density bound]\label{thm:densitybal}
If in addition $p \le q \le 3p$, then
\[ \frac{S(H,pq)}{pq} \le \frac{4h}{\sqrt{pq}}. \]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Write $t = \sqrt{q/p} \in [1,\sqrt3]$; then $(p+q)/\sqrt{pq} = t + 1/t \le \sqrt3 + 1/\sqrt3 = 4/\sqrt3 < 4$. Hence $p + q \le 4\sqrt{pq}$, and $S \le h(p+q) \le 4h\sqrt{pq}$ by Corollary~\ref{cor:tiny}; divide by $pq$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Expected evaluations, lower bound]\label{thm:trialslow}
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:densitybal}, with $N = pq$ and $S = S(H,N) > 0$,
\[ \frac NS \ge \frac{\sqrt N}{4h}. \]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Rearrange Theorem~\ref{thm:densitybal}. (If $h = 0$ then $H = 1$, which has no roots, so $S = 0$ and the hypothesis fails.)
\end{proof}

Interpreting $S/N$ as the probability that a uniformly random $j_0 \in [0,N)$ succeeds, $N/S$ is the expected geometric waiting time. Thus a uniformly random evaluation point succeeds with probability at most $4h/\sqrt N$, and one expects at least $\sqrt N/(4h)$ evaluations.

A natural objection is that a real attacker uses many discriminants. This does not help.

\begin{definition}
For a finite family $F \subset \Z[X]$ put $\mathcal P(F,N) := \{(G,j) \in F \times [0,N) : \eg(G,j,N)\ \text{is a nontrivial divisor of } N\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Multi-discriminant barrier]\label{thm:multi}
Let $N = pq$ be balanced and $F$ a nonempty finite family of monic polynomials each of degree at most $h$. Then
\[ \frac{|\mathcal P(F,N)|}{|F| \cdot N} \le \frac{4h}{\sqrt N}. \]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The fibre of $\mathcal P(F,N)$ over $G \in F$ is contained in $\{G\}\times\mathcal S(G,N)$, so $|\mathcal P(F,N)| \le \sum_{G\in F} S(G,N)$. Each term is at most $(4\deg G/\sqrt N)N \le (4h/\sqrt N)N$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:densitybal} and monotonicity in the degree. Sum over the $|F|$ members and divide by $|F|\cdot N$.
\end{proof}

Thus the search space may be enlarged in the discriminant direction as much as one wishes; the density of useful pairs is unchanged. The barrier is not an artifact of using a single $H_D$.

\section{Sharpness: the method really is $\Theta(\sqrt N)$}

A lower bound on running time is compatible with the method never working, so we prove matching upper bounds.

\begin{theorem}[Sharpness of the counting bound]\label{thm:sharp}
Suppose $r_p = r_q = h$ with $h \le p$ and $h \le q$ --- the generic complete-splitting case at two primes both represented by forms of discriminant $D$. Then
\[ S(H,pq) + 2h^2 = h(p+q), \]
so Corollary~\ref{cor:tiny} is tight up to the second-order term $2h^2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Substitute $r_p = r_q = h$ into Theorem~\ref{thm:count}: $S = h(q-h) + (p-h)h = h(p+q) - 2h^2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Roots modulo $p$ only]\label{thm:onesided}
If $r_q = 0$ then $S(H,pq) = r_p \cdot q$; in particular $S \ge q$ whenever $r_p \ge 1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:count} with $r_q = 0$ gives $S = r_p q$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Expected evaluations, upper bound]\label{thm:trialsup}
If $p \le q$, $r_p \ge 1$ and $r_q = 0$, then $N/S \le p \le \sqrt N$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:onesided}, $S \ge q$, so $N/S \le pq/q = p$; and $p \le q$ gives $p^2 \le pq$, i.e.\ $p \le \sqrt{pq}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Two-sided $\sqrt N$ scaling]\label{thm:twosided}
Let $N = pq$ be a balanced semiprime and $H$ monic of degree $h$ with $r_p \ge 1$ and $r_q = 0$. Then
\[ \frac{\sqrt N}{4h} \le \frac{N}{S(H,N)} \le \sqrt N. \]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Combine Theorems~\ref{thm:trialslow} (whose hypothesis $S>0$ holds by Theorem~\ref{thm:onesided}) and~\ref{thm:trialsup}.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The class number cancels}

\begin{theorem}[Total work barrier]\label{thm:work}
Let $N = pq$ be balanced, $H$ monic of degree $h$, and $S = S(H,N) > 0$. Then
\[ h \cdot \frac NS \ge \frac{\sqrt N}{4}. \]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $h = 0$ then $H = 1$ has no roots modulo any prime, so $S = 0$, contrary to hypothesis. For $h \ge 1$, multiply Theorem~\ref{thm:trialslow} by $h > 0$.
\end{proof}

Evaluating a degree-$h$ polynomial costs at least $h$ ring multiplications; Horner's rule achieves exactly $h$ multiplications and $h$ additions and is optimal for a generic polynomial. Hence $h\cdot(N/S)$ is a lower bound for the total arithmetic work, and Theorem~\ref{thm:work} bounds it below by $\sqrt N/4$ \emph{with no $h$ in the bound}: the factor $1/h$ gained in the trial count is exactly repaid by the cost of one trial. The $\sqrt N$ rate is a conservation law of the construction.

There is also a quantitative ceiling on $h$: by standard class number bounds $h(D) = O(\sqrt{|D|}\log|D|)$, so $h \approx \sqrt N$ requires $|D| \gtrsim N/\log^2 N$ and a class polynomial of degree $\sqrt N$ with coefficients of $\Omega(\sqrt N)$ bits --- an object that cannot be written down, let alone evaluated.

\subsection{Reparametrisation does not help}

\begin{theorem}[Composition barrier]\label{thm:comp}
Let $H$ and $g$ be monic with $\deg g = d \ge 1$. Then $H \circ g$ is monic of degree $hd$ and
\[ \frac{S(H\circ g,\, pq)}{pq} \le hd\Bigl(\frac1p + \frac1q\Bigr). \]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Monicity and $\deg(H\circ g) = \deg H \cdot \deg g$ are standard; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:density} to $H \circ g$.
\end{proof}

Substituting $j_0 \mapsto g(j_0)$ is the natural way to try to ``aim'' at the structured set; the density bound degrades by exactly the factor $d$ by which the per-evaluation cost grows.

\section{The circularity bottleneck: no precomputation}

The useful set is $\{j_0 : H_D(j_0) \equiv 0 \bmod p\}$, defined in terms of the unknown prime. The only apparent escape is offline precomputation: fix a table $T$ of evaluation points and a family $F$ of discriminants once and for all, and hope some entry hits.

\begin{definition}[Catchable primes]
For $H \in \Z[X]$ and finite $T \subset \Z$,
\[ \Catch(H,T) := \bigcup_{t \in T}\{\text{prime factors of } |H(t)|\}. \]
Equivalently, $r \in \Catch(H,T)$ iff there is $t \in T$ with $H(t) \ne 0$ and $r \mid H(t)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Precomputation bound]\label{thm:precomp}
For any $H$ and finite $T$,
\[ |\Catch(H,T)| \le \sum_{t\in T} \log_2 |H(t)|. \]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
A union bound gives $|\Catch(H,T)| \le \sum_{t}\omega(|H(t)|)$, where $\omega$ counts distinct prime factors. For $n \ne 0$ the radical divides $n$ and each prime factor is at least $2$, so $2^{\omega(n)} \le \prod_{r \mid n} r \le n$, whence $\omega(n) \le \log_2 n$.
\end{proof}

The right-hand side is determined by the \emph{bit size of the table}, with no dependence on the modulus $N$ under attack: a table of $B$ bits catches $O(B)$ primes.

\begin{corollary}[Infinitely many invisible primes]\label{cor:invisible}
For any $H$ and finite $T$, the set of primes outside $\Catch(H,T)$ is infinite.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
$\Catch(H,T)$ is finite and there are infinitely many primes.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Precomputed attacks fail on arbitrarily large inputs]\label{thm:tablefails}
Let $F$ be a finite family of polynomials, $T$ a finite table of evaluation points, and $M$ any bound. Then there exist distinct primes $p, q > M$ such that for every $G \in F$ and every $t \in T$, $\eg(G,t,pq)$ is not a nontrivial divisor of $pq$; and if moreover $G(t) \ne 0$ for all such pairs, then $\eg(G,t,pq) = 1$ for all of them.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $\Sigma := \bigcup_{G \in F}\Catch(G,T)$, a finite set of primes. Choose a prime $p > M$ with $p \notin \Sigma$, then a prime $q > p$ with $q \notin \Sigma$; both exist by Corollary~\ref{cor:invisible}. Fix $G \in F$ and $t \in T$. If $G(t) = 0$ then $\gcd(0,pq) = pq$, not a nontrivial divisor. Otherwise $p \notin \Catch(G,T)$ gives $p \nmid G(t)$ and likewise $q \nmid G(t)$, so the gcd is $1$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:gcd}.
\end{proof}

The structured set cannot be enumerated in advance, only searched --- and Theorem~\ref{thm:trialslow} prices that search at $\sqrt N/(4h)$ evaluations, Theorem~\ref{thm:work} at $\sqrt N/4$ units of work.

\section{Asymptotic classification}

Let $x = \log N$ and define the proven cost profile of the method for fixed $h > 0$:
\[ C_h(x) := \frac{e^{x/2}}{4h}, \]
which is exactly $\sqrt N/(4h)$.

\begin{lemma}[Stability]\label{lem:stab}
If $f$ is superpolynomial and $c > 0$ then $x \mapsto f(x)/c$ is superpolynomial. If $f$ is subexponential and $c \in \R$ then $x \mapsto c f(x)$ is subexponential.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Ladder placement]\label{thm:ladder}
For every $h > 0$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $C_h$ is superpolynomial; in particular it is not polynomially bounded, so the method is not polynomial-time.
\item $C_h$ is not subexponential; it does not reach the sieve rung $L_N[1/3,c]$.
\item Eventually $e^{x/4} \le C_h(x)$: the cost dominates the birthday barrier of Pollard's rho.
\item The sieve barrier $L_N[1/3,c]$ is subexponential, so $C_h$ and the sieve cost lie on strictly different rungs, with $C_h$ on the worse one.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
(1) $x^d/C_h(x) = 4h\,x^d e^{-x/2} \to 0$ for every $d$, using Lemma~\ref{lem:stab}. (2) If $C_h$ were subexponential then so would be $4h\,C_h(x) = e^{x/2}$, contradicting Definition~\ref{def:cost} at $\varepsilon = 1/4$ since $e^{x/2}/e^{x/4} = e^{x/4}\to\infty$. (3) Write $e^{x/2} = e^{x/4}e^{x/4}$; as $e^{x/4}\to\infty$, eventually $e^{x/4} \ge 4h$, whence $e^{x/2} \ge 4h\,e^{x/4}$, i.e.\ $e^{x/4} \le C_h(x)$. (4) $L_N[1/3,c] = \exp\bigl((c+o(1))x^{1/3}(\log x)^{2/3}\bigr)$ and $x^{1/3}(\log x)^{2/3}/(\varepsilon x)\to 0$ for every $\varepsilon>0$.
\end{proof}

Summarising:
\[ \underbrace{L_N[1/3,c]}_{\text{number field sieve}} \prec \underbrace{N^{1/4}}_{\text{Pollard rho}} \preceq \underbrace{\sqrt N/(4h)}_{\text{singular moduli}} \prec \underbrace{N}_{\text{trial division}}. \]

Singular moduli factoring is limited by the same resource as the collision methods --- the inability to sample a set defined in terms of the unknown factor better than at random --- and is therefore not a new resource, merely a new instance of an old barrier.

