\section{Algorithms}

\subsection{The method}

\begin{quote}\ttfamily\small
INPUT\ \ N (semiprime), discriminants D[1..k], budget B\\
OUTPUT\ a nontrivial factor of N, or FAIL\\[2pt]
for j0 = 0, 1, 2, ...:\\
\hspace*{2em}for each D:\\
\hspace*{4em}v <- H\_D(j0) mod N\ \ \ \ \# Horner: deg(H\_D) multiplications\\
\hspace*{4em}g <- gcd(v, N)\\
\hspace*{4em}if 1 < g < N: return g\ \ \# g = N means a CM point mod BOTH primes\\
\hspace*{4em}if evaluations > B: return FAIL
\end{quote}

One iteration costs $O(h)$ modular multiplications plus one gcd, i.e.\ $\tilde O(h \log N)$ bit operations. By Theorem~\ref{thm:trialslow} the expected iteration count is at least $\sqrt N/(4h)$, and by Theorem~\ref{thm:work} the expected total is at least $\sqrt N/4$ modular multiplications.

\subsection{Exact success count}

\begin{quote}\ttfamily\small
INPUT\ \ H, distinct primes p, q\\
OUTPUT\ S = \#\{ j in [0,pq) : gcd(H(j), pq) nontrivial \}\\[2pt]
r\_p <- \#\{ x in Z/p : H(x) = 0 \}\\
r\_q <- \#\{ x in Z/q : H(x) = 0 \}\\
return r\_p*(q - r\_q) + (p - r\_p)*r\_q
\end{quote}

Cost $O((p+q)h)$ naively, or $\tilde O(h\log p + h \log q)$ via $\gcd(X^p - X, H)$ --- versus $\Theta(pq)$ for brute-force enumeration, and exact.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{What the analysis does and does not show}

The analysis is unusually clean because Theorems~\ref{thm:criterion} and~\ref{thm:count} use nothing about $H_D$ beyond its being an integer polynomial. There is no heuristic step, no equidistribution assumption, no Riemann Hypothesis. The only input from complex multiplication is qualitative: it explains \emph{why} $H_D$ has roots modulo $p$ when it does, and hence when the favourable configuration of Theorem~\ref{thm:twosided} arises.

This is a strength and a limitation. Strength: the conclusion is unconditional and applies to every polynomial-evaluation-plus-gcd scheme. Limitation: precisely because the argument ignores the arithmetic of $H_D$, it cannot rule out a method that uses that arithmetic differently --- for instance one computing with CM curves over $\Z/N$ rather than merely evaluating an integer polynomial.

\subsection{The pattern behind $\sqrt N$ methods}

Pollard's rho exploits collision of a random walk modulo $p$ after $O(\sqrt p)$ steps, but cannot observe the walk modulo $p$; Pollard's $p-1$ exploits smoothness of $p-1$, but cannot see which primes have smooth predecessor; the elliptic curve method exploits smooth group orders over $\F_p$ and pays subexponentially only because it may \emph{resample the group}. Singular moduli factoring exploits that CM points modulo $p$ form a set of size $h$ inside $\F_p$ --- again invisible from outside.

In each case a genuine arithmetic structure is converted into a search for a set defined in terms of the secret, and each search is priced at the square root of the modulus. Theorem~\ref{thm:tablefails} makes this precise here: the structured set cannot be tabulated offline at any finite size. The number field sieve escapes the pattern because the structure it exploits --- smooth values of an integer polynomial over $\Z$ --- is visible without knowing $p$, and the pieces are assembled by linear algebra rather than by search.

\subsection{Practical consequences}

For a $2048$-bit modulus, $\sqrt N = 2^{1024}$ whereas the sieve heuristic is around $2^{110}$. A class number of $2^{40}$ --- far beyond what is representable --- changes $\sqrt N/(4h)$ only to $2^{982}$, and Theorem~\ref{thm:work} leaves the work at $2^{1022}$. Singular moduli factoring is therefore of no practical threat; its interest is structural. One practical corollary remains: Theorem~\ref{thm:count} is a useful tool for auditing claims about CM-based attacks, since any proposal in this family beating density $4h/\sqrt N$ must violate either Lagrange's theorem or the Chinese Remainder Theorem.

\subsection{The role of blindness}

The statement ``$H_D$ mod $p$ has $h$ roots'' holds only when $D$ is a square modulo $p$; for roughly half of the discriminants it fails, and then $H_D$ may be irreducible modulo $p$. When it fails at both primes, the success probability is exactly $0$. A correct expected-cost analysis therefore has two layers: the cost of finding a discriminant usable for the unknown $p$, and, conditional on that, the $\Theta(\sqrt N)$ cost of Theorem~\ref{thm:twosided}. The first layer is itself circular, which is why one sweeps over discriminants in practice and why Theorem~\ref{thm:multi} is the right statement for a realistic attacker.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Degree--density duality: the barrier is a conservation law]
For every family of integer polynomials $H_k$ with $\deg H_k = h_k$ and every family of evaluation strategies, the product (density of useful points) $\times$ (cost of one evaluation) is $O(1/\sqrt N)$: the two factors trade off exactly. Formally, if $W(H)$ is any cost measure with $W(H) \ge \deg H$, then $W(H)\,S(H,N)/N \le 4W(H)h/\sqrt N$ cannot be improved to $o(1/\sqrt N)$ by any choice of $H$, because the bound $S \le h(p+q)$ is sharp (Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp}).
\end{conjecture}

The key insight is that $h$ appears in the numerator of the density bound and in the denominator of the per-evaluation cost with the same exponent, so the $\sqrt N$ rate is a conservation law of the construction, not a limitation of the counting technique. Theorem~\ref{thm:work} already proves the $\sqrt N/4$ lower bound for a single monic $H$; the missing step is an abstract cost model with $W(H) \ge \deg H$ and the statement $\forall H,\ W(H)\cdot N/S(H,N) \ge \sqrt N/4$.

\begin{conjecture}[Elliptic escape: CM torsion evades the counting bound]
Replacing ``evaluate $H_D$ at $j_0$'' by ``compute the $\ell$-division polynomial of a random curve $E/\Z_N$ and take a gcd of its value'' gives a success density bounded by $c\ell^2/\sqrt N$: the same $\sqrt N$ rate with $h$ replaced by the degree $(\ell^2-1)/2$, hence still no gain; but the density is achieved for a positive proportion of curves rather than a proportion $\approx h/p$.
\end{conjecture}

Both methods are instances of one scheme: a fixed integer polynomial whose roots modulo $p$ encode an arithmetic property of $p$. Theorem~\ref{thm:count} applies verbatim, since it never uses anything about $H_D$ beyond its degree. Only the sharpness direction (existence of roots) needs new input, namely Hasse's bound.

\begin{conjecture}[Adaptive search does not beat uniform search]
For every adaptive deterministic algorithm that may choose its next evaluation point as a function of all previously observed gcds, the number of evaluations needed on a worst-case balanced semiprime is still $\Omega(\sqrt N/h)$.
\end{conjecture}

A failed trial returns the single bit ``$\gcd = 1$'', excluding only the $O(h)$ residues modulo $p$ and modulo $q$ that the trial tested; an adversary argument in the style of Theorem~\ref{thm:tablefails} should show that any transcript of $o(\sqrt N/h)$ failures is consistent with many semiprimes. Theorem~\ref{thm:tablefails} is the non-adaptive case, obtained by taking $T$ to be the algorithm's fixed query set.

Further targets: quantify blindness (the proportion of semiprimes on which every member of a bounded discriminant family is blind, and hence the true two-layer expected cost); sharpen the balance constant (for $q \le \lambda p$ the optimal constant is $\sqrt\lambda + 1/\sqrt\lambda$, giving density $\le (2+o(1))h/\sqrt N$ as $\lambda \to 1$); prove second-moment concentration around Theorem~\ref{thm:twosided}; and extend Theorem~\ref{thm:count} by inclusion--exclusion to $N$ with $k$ prime factors, where the barrier should become $\min_i p_i$ rather than $\sqrt N$.

\section{Conclusion}

Singular moduli factoring works, exactly as complex multiplication suggests it should. Its analysis, however, admits no heuristic wiggle room: the success criterion is an exact exclusive-or, the success count is an exact Chinese-Remainder identity, Lagrange's theorem caps the useful set at $h(p+q)$ out of $pq$ points, and the expected number of evaluations on a balanced semiprime lies provably in $[\sqrt N/(4h), \sqrt N]$. The class-number speed-up is exactly cancelled by evaluation cost, reparametrisation is exactly cancelled by degree growth, running many discriminants leaves the density unchanged, and no finite precomputed table helps on more than a bounded set of primes. The cost profile $e^{x/2}/(4h)$ is superpolynomial and genuinely exponential, dominating the birthday barrier and lying strictly above the subexponential sieve rung. Singular moduli factoring is a beautiful, fully analysable member of the $\sqrt N$ family, and its barrier is the familiar one: a structured set indexed by the secret is no easier to find than the secret itself.

